# XSH realloc() and reallocarray()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Both functions are on one page. A zero-size request is obsolescent
# behaviour (OB) with two outcomes; whether a null return from it leaves the
# old block allocated is not stated, so the tests never free a block after a
# null result of a zero-size request.

assertion  XSH.realloc.001
anchor     functions/realloc.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       realloc(NULL, n) acts as malloc(n): a non-null, fundamentally
           aligned pointer to n usable bytes.
test       t/001-null-ptr.c

assertion  XSH.realloc.002
anchor     functions/realloc.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/realloc.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       realloc() to a larger and then to a smaller non-zero size keeps
           the old contents up to the smaller of the two sizes, and every
           returned pointer is fundamentally aligned.
test       t/002-grow-shrink.c

assertion  XSH.realloc.003
anchor     functions/realloc.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/realloc.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/realloc.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      1988
says       realloc(p, SIZE_MAX) returns a null pointer and sets errno to
           ENOMEM, and the old block is not deallocated: its contents are
           unchanged and it can still be resized and freed.
test       t/003-failure-keeps-old.c

assertion  XSH.realloc.004
anchor     functions/realloc.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       realloc(p, 0) with p from malloc() either returns a null pointer
           with errno EINVAL, or returns a pointer (to be freed) after which
           the old block is gone.
note       The behaviour is the obsolescent one the page describes; in the
           null case the test does not free p, since it is not stated
           whether p was released.
test       t/004-zero-size.c

assertion  XSH.realloc.005
anchor     functions/realloc.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       reallocarray(NULL, n, m) returns usable aligned space for n*m
           bytes, and reallocarray(p, n, m) resizes keeping the old contents
           up to the smaller size, like realloc(p, n*m).
test       t/005-reallocarray.c

assertion  XSH.realloc.006
anchor     functions/realloc.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/realloc.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2024
says       When nelem * elsize overflows, reallocarray() returns a null
           pointer, sets errno to ENOMEM, and leaves the old block intact.
test       t/006-reallocarray-overflow.c

assertion  XSH.realloc.007
anchor     functions/realloc.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       reallocarray(p, 0, m) and reallocarray(p, n, 0) behave like a
           zero-size realloc(): a null pointer with errno EINVAL, or a
           pointer after which the old block is gone.
test       t/007-reallocarray-zero.c
